  <preface xml:id="preface-about">
    <title>About This Text</title>

    <p>
      This text is for incoming graduate students preparing for MAT 5610.
      Some of you are refreshing real analysis you have studied before;
      others are meeting analysis-style proof for the first time. All
      students do all of the work. The difference in starting points
      appears in one place: the orientation of each module has a note on
      pacing that suggests where to spend your time.
    </p>

    <p>
      The nine modules begin with mathematical language and proof
      strategies (<xref ref="ch-mod1"/>), the real number system
      (<xref ref="ch-mod2"/>), and sets, functions, and cardinality
      (<xref ref="ch-mod3"/>). They continue with sequences
      (<xref ref="ch-mod4"/>), series (<xref ref="ch-mod5"/>), limits,
      continuity, and uniform continuity (<xref ref="ch-mod6"/>), differentiation
      (<xref ref="ch-mod7"/>), and the Riemann integral
      (<xref ref="ch-mod8"/>), and close with sequences and series of
      functions (<xref ref="ch-mod9"/>). Later modules use earlier ones, so
      work through them in order.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every module has six components. Use them in this order.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Orientation.</alert> The module's objectives, ungraded
            self-assessment questions, and a road map with an estimated
            time for each part of the module.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Companion Reading and Study Guide.</alert> Guided questions to answer with the
            assigned textbook sections open in front of you.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Worked Examples.</alert> Complete proofs that model the
            standard expected of your own writing, with scratch work kept
            separate from the formal proof.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Practice Problem Set.</alert> Problems to work with
            complete written solutions. They are not submitted.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Bridge Reading Guide.</alert> A reading in the MAT 5610 text,
            with guided questions, showing how the module's material looks
            there.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>Assessment.</alert> Proofs and a short reflection,
            submitted for feedback.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Practice problems are labeled <m>[\mathbf{F}]</m> (foundational),
      <m>[\mathbf{S}]</m> (standard), or <m>[\mathbf{C}]</m> (challenge).
      The labels describe difficulty, not importance: problems at every
      level are part of the module.
    </p>

    <p>
      Many exercises carry a hint, an answer, or a solution. In the online
      version these are hidden until you open them; in the PDF they follow
      the exercise. In either format, try the problem before you look.
      Assessment items have no answers or solutions, and their hints point
      only to an approach.
    </p>

    <p>
      The study guides follow Zorn's <pubtitle>Understanding Real
      Analysis</pubtitle> and, in <xref ref="ch-mod1"/> and
      <xref ref="ch-mod3"/>, Hirst. The bridge readings connect each module
      to Bauldry, the text for MAT 5610. This text cites these books and,
      apart from a few short quotations, does not reproduce them, so you
      will need your own copies.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each module's road map lists lightboard videos. The videos are
      distributed through the course and are not part of this text.
      Assessments are also submitted through the course.
    </p>

    <p>
      Read the
      <url href="https://morrisqa.github.io/analysis-prep/" visual="morrisqa.github.io/analysis-prep">web version of this text</url>
      or download the
      <url href="https://morrisqa.github.io/analysis-prep/book.pdf" visual="morrisqa.github.io/analysis-prep/book.pdf">PDF version of this text</url>.
    </p>
  </preface>
</frontmatter>
